course: world
lesson: 174
title: The YouTuber who is right about everything
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Jack's favourite YouTuber is brilliant at Minecraft. In one video he says, "Vegetables are a scam, just eat protein." Jack stops eating vegetables. But being an expert at building castles doesn't make someone an expert on food. Doctors and dietitians agree vegetables matter for health.
explain: Liking someone a lot can make everything they say sound right; grown-ups call that the halo effect. Leo's friend says a YouTuber "is right about everything, so the flat Earth thing is probably true". He's right about Fortnite. He's not NASA.
explain: Ask two questions. Does this person really know about this topic? And do they get something from saying it? A streamer with a drink logo in every video is paid by that drink; a gamer who trashes one phone may be sponsored by a different phone company.
explain: Big numbers don't settle it either: ten million subscribers don't make a fact true, and one famous person who got rich without finishing school doesn't show school is useless; we just don't see the thousands who didn't get rich.
explain: Family mockery aims at the blind trust, not the fan: "Your YouTuber builds great castles, but he's not a dietitian, love. Eat your carrots."
fact: Being an expert in one thing, like a game, does not make someone an expert in another, like food or science; grown-ups call trusting them anyway the appeal to the expert who isn't.
fact: The halo effect is when liking a person makes us believe everything they say.
fact: When someone is paid to praise a product, their opinion may not be honest; grown-ups call this a conflict of interest.
fact: Videos that are paid adverts are often marked "ad" or "sponsored", sometimes in small print.
fact: Energy drinks are not recommended for children because of the caffeine in them.
fact: The Earth is round: we know from photos from space, ships disappearing hull-first over the horizon, and people sailing and flying all the way round it.
fact: Seeing only the famous success stories and not the many who failed is called survivorship bias; on average, people with more education earn more and have more choices.
fact: Lemon water is fine to drink, but it does not burn fat, and lots of lemon acid can wear away enamel, the hard outer layer of your teeth; health comes from eating well, moving and sleeping.
fact: Teachers study for years; like anyone they can be wrong sometimes, but "they don't know anything" is clearly false.
fact: Lots of followers doesn't make a claim true; sharks have a very good sense of smell, but "one drop of blood from miles away" is an exaggeration.
fact: Calling every mistake "a joke" means the person can never be wrong; grown-ups call that moving the goalposts.
fact: "Best game ever" is an opinion, a matter of taste, not a fact; watching gameplay or trying a demo helps you decide.
fact: Words people use: "a scam" is a trick to fool people, "trash" means rubbish, "influencer" is someone paid or followed online for their opinions, "streamer" is someone who plays games live online, "NASA" is America's space agency, and "a dietitian" is an expert on food and health.
exercise: Jack's Minecraft YouTuber says vegetables are a scam. What is the broken step? | Being good at Minecraft doesn't make him a food expert | Minecraft experts know a lot about food | Protein is the only food people need | Vegetables really are a scam, because farmers just want to sell more of them
exercise: What do doctors and dietitians say about vegetables? | They are important for health | They are a scam | They are only for adults | They can be replaced by protein shakes
exercise: Mia notices the energy drink logo in every one of the streamer's videos. What does that suggest? | The streamer is probably paid by the drink company | The drink must really be the best | The streamer bought the drink himself | Logos have to appear on every single video, because the law says so
exercise: Why are energy drinks not recommended for children? | They contain caffeine | They are too sweet to drink | They are only sold abroad | They make you grow too fast
exercise: Toby says a YouTuber is right about everything, so the flat Earth joke is probably true. What trap is this? | The halo effect | The sunk cost fallacy | Collective punishment | The gambler's fallacy
exercise: How do we know the Earth is round? | Photos from space, ships vanishing hull-first, and trips all the way round | Because a YouTuber said so | Because maps are drawn round | Because the Sun looks round
exercise: An influencer says you don't need school because she didn't finish and is rich. What is missing? | The thousands who skipped school and are not rich | Proof that she is really rich, like photos of her house and her cars | A list of her favourite subjects | The name of her old school
exercise: Kai's favourite gamer calls a phone trash. What is important to know? | The gamer is sponsored by a different phone company | The gamer has tested every phone | The phone really is trash, because a famous gamer has said that it is | Gamers never get paid
exercise: Sophie's aunt drank lemon water for a month with no change. What is true? | Lemon water doesn't burn fat | It needed two months to work | She drank it at the wrong time | It works only with sugar
exercise: Oscar's YouTuber says teachers don't know anything. What is true? | Teachers study for years, though anyone can be wrong sometimes | Teachers know nothing at all | YouTubers know more than teachers | Teachers are always right about everything, because they went to university
exercise: Amira says 10 million subscribers can't be wrong about sharks. What is true? | Followers don't make a claim true; "miles away" is an exaggeration | Sharks smell blood from hundreds of miles | Ten million people checked the fact | Sharks have no sense of smell
exercise: Finn's YouTuber was shown to be wrong, and Finn says it was a joke. What is Finn doing? | Calling every mistake a joke so he can never be wrong | Checking the facts carefully | Admitting that his YouTuber made a mistake, and checking the next video | Asking an expert for help
exercise: Harper bought the "best game of all time" and found it boring. Why? | "Best" is an opinion, a matter of taste | The game was broken when it arrived at home | She played it the wrong way from the start | The gamer was lying on purpose to sell it
exercise: What two questions help with any online claim? | Does this person know the topic, and do they gain from saying it? | How many likes, and how loud is it? | Is it funny, and is it short? | Is the person young and famous, and do lots of my friends follow them too?
exercise: What do grown-ups call it when someone is paid to praise what they talk about? | A conflict of interest | The halo effect | A fair review | Survivorship bias
test: Jack's mum: "Your YouTuber builds great castles, but he's not a dietitian, love." What does "dietitian" mean? | An expert on food and health | A builder of castles | A YouTube manager | A type of game
test: Her big sister: "He's sponsored, Mia. If they paid him to say socks are tasty, he'd say socks are tasty." What is she pointing out? | Paid opinions may not be honest | Socks really are tasty | The streamer loves energy drinks | Sponsors only pay for honest views
test: Leo: "He's right about Fortnite, Toby. He's not NASA." What is his point? | Being right about games doesn't make him right about space | NASA plays Fortnite | Fortnite proves the Earth is flat | The YouTuber secretly works for NASA, so he must know about space
test: Ellie's dad: "For every famous one, there's thousands who aren't on YouTube. We don't see them." What trap is he naming? | Seeing only the success stories | Spending more because you spent | Changing the rules after losing | Blaming the controller
test: Dad: "He works for the other brand, Kai. That's like asking a cat if dogs are any good." What should Kai do? | Read reviews from many places before deciding | Trust the gamer even more, because he's honest | Buy the very phone that the gamer says he hates | Ask the family cat for its advice about phones too
test: Aunt: "A month of lemons and the only thing I've lost is the enamel on my teeth." What does she mean? | Lemon water didn't burn fat, and acid can harm teeth | She actually lost all of her extra weight from her teeth | Lemons only start to work properly after a whole month | She simply drank far too little lemon water for it to work
test: His teacher: "Interesting. And where did your YouTuber learn to read, Oscar?" Is the teacher being nasty to Oscar? | No, she's gently showing the claim can't be right | Yes, she's calling Oscar stupid | Yes, she hates YouTubers | No, she just agrees with him that teachers don't really know anything
test: Her cousin: "10 million people also follow a cat that plays the piano. Doesn't make the cat a professor." What trap is he mocking? | Many followers means right | A dream as proof | Silence means yes | One lucky try
test: Finn's brother: "Every time he's wrong, it's a joke? He's the funniest man alive then." What would a trustworthy YouTuber do? | Say "I was wrong" and correct it | Call every mistake a joke | Delete the video and say nothing | Blame his viewers
test: Mum: "He likes it. You don't. Both of you are allowed. It's a game, not a law." What is true? | "Best game ever" is an opinion, not a fact | The game must be good, because the gamer said so | Harper's opinion about the game is just wrong | Only famous people are allowed to judge games
test: What would happen if Jack listened to a doctor about food and to the YouTuber about Minecraft? | He'd get good advice on both, from people who know | He'd get worse advice on both | He'd have to stop playing Minecraft | He'd have to stop eating vegetables, since the YouTuber knows best about food
exam: Identify the principal error in accepting advice on nutrition from a person whose expertise lies in a building game. | Expertise in one field is treated as expertise in another | The game was insufficiently popular | The advice was delivered in a video rather than in a printed book | The person was too young
exam: Determine the term properly applied to the circumstance in which a reviewer is remunerated by a competitor of the product reviewed. | A conflict of interest | The halo effect | Survivorship bias | Circular reasoning
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the shape of the Earth. | It is round, as shown by photographs from space and circumnavigation | It is flat, as asserted in certain videos | Its shape has not been determined | It is round only at the equator
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the assertion that a claim is true because its author has ten million subscribers? | An appeal to popularity which does not establish truth | A scientific verification of the claim | A fair sample of expert opinion | A guarantee of accuracy issued by the platform on which it appears
